-- title:
--   Chevalley's theorem on constructible sets
-- statement:
--   **Chevalley's theorem on constructible sets.** Let $f:R\to S$ be a ring homomorphism of finite presentation. Then the induced map $\operatorname{Spec}S\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ sends constructible sets to constructible sets: if $s\subseteq\operatorname{Spec}S$ is constructible, so is its image.
--
--   Constructible sets are the finite unions of locally closed sets (in the Noetherian case), and Chevalley's theorem is the algebraic form of the fact that the image of a polynomial map is described by finitely many polynomial equations and inequations. It underlies quantifier elimination for algebraically closed fields and is a basic tool in algebraic geometry.
--
--   **Formalization note.** Mathlib's `PrimeSpectrum.isConstructible_comap_image`. `RingHom.FinitePresentation` means $S$ is a finitely presented $R$-algebra via $f$. `Topology.IsConstructible` is the general topological notion of constructibility (the Boolean algebra generated by retrocompact open sets), which agrees with the classical one on Noetherian spectra.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's curated theorem manifests (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib as `PrimeSpectrum.isConstructible_comap_image`. Proof here reduces to that Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

theorem chevalley_constructible_image {R S : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] {f : R →+* S} (hf : f.FinitePresentation)
    {s : Set (PrimeSpectrum S)} (hs : Topology.IsConstructible s) :
    Topology.IsConstructible (PrimeSpectrum.comap f '' s) := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
